  "id": "pca9685-breakout",
  "name": "PCA9685 16-channel PWM / servo driver (Adafruit-style breakout)",
  "category": "driver",
  "description": "Drives up to 16 servos or LEDs over I2C (2 pins), so the microcontroller does not have to make the PWM itself.",
  "datasheet": "https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/data-sheet/PCA9685.pdf",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.nxp.com/docs/en/data-sheet/PCA9685.pdf",
    "https://learn.adafruit.com/16-channel-pwm-servo-driver/pinouts",
    "https://www.adafruit.com/product/815",
    "https://learn.adafruit.com/16-channel-pwm-servo-driver.md?view=all"
  ],
  "confidence": "verified",
  "notes": [
    "Two separate supplies: VCC is the chip logic (2.3-5.5 V, from your microcontroller), V+ is servo power (5-6 V, from a supply able to give the servos stall current). Never connect VCC to V+.",
    "Inputs (SDA, SCL, OE, address pins) are 5.5 V tolerant, but HIGH needs at least 0.7 x VCC. With VCC = 5 V a 3.3 V microcontroller is below that threshold: power VCC from 3.3 V when using an ESP32 or Pico.",
    "SDA/SCL have 10 kOhm pull-ups to VCC on the Adafruit board; several boards on one bus add up their pull-ups in parallel.",
    "Default I2C address 0x40 (A0-A5 solder jumpers change it). PWM frequency for servos: 50 Hz.",
    "Each output has a 220 Ohm series resistor and swings to VCC: fine for servo signals and LEDs, not for driving motors.",
    "The terminal block input is reverse-polarity protected; the V+ header pins are not. Adafruit suggests 5-6 V for servos and a bulk capacitor (about 100 uF per servo, footprint C2) for many servos.",
    "Clone boards (often blue/purple) use the same pinout; some omit the reverse-polarity MOSFET. Check yours."
  ],
